   $q$A reader points out that Brandt studied only sixty players for one season. Which revision of sentence 12 best accounts for this limit while keeping the writer's claim?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Brandt's study proves that every athlete in every sport must stop using still stretches forever.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overgeneralization"},{"text":"Although Brandt studied only sixty players for one season, the results give coaches a strong reason to try moving warm-ups.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Because Brandt's study was so small, its results are useless, and coaches should ignore them completely.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"dismisses-evidence"},{"text":"Brandt's team followed sixty high school soccer players and split them into two equal groups.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"evidence-without-conclusion"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Although Brandt studied only sixty players for one season, the results give coaches a strong reason to try moving warm-ups.$q$, $q$The correct revision admits the study's size but still draws a reasonable conclusion from its results. Calling the results useless throws away real evidence instead of weighing it.$q$, 'draft', 'bd5f01da-1909-5142-a91a-1d328e26d8ef')
